Google Fi Wireless is not subject to data traffic deprioritization during times of high network usage. and do you go along to get along or say yes when you really want to say no? We've all done it, agreed to a volunteer position that we didn't really have the time for, or held our tongue at the holiday dinner table to avoid a family fight, or kept quiet at work when speaking up could have helped a colleague avoid a mistake. Today we're going to talk about the psychology of speaking up and saying no. Why is that so hard to do?
Google Fi Wireless 在网络高负荷使用期间不会遭遇数据流量降速。你会为了合群而选择顺从，还是在真正想说“不”的时候说“好”呢？我们都做过这种事，比如答应一个自己其实没时间去做的志愿者岗位，或者在节日晚宴上闭口不言以避免家庭争吵，或者在工作时保持沉默，而当时直言本可以帮同事避免一个错误。今天我们要讨论的是直言不讳和拒绝的心理学。为什么这件事这么难做到？

3
00:00:59.000 --> 00:01:49.000
What does it mean to defy, to comply, to compromise? comply or to consent. And how can you strengthen your resolve and ability to say no when the situation calls for it? Welcome to Speaking of Psychology, the flagship podcast of the American Psychological Association that examines the links between psychological science and everyday life. I'm Kim Mills. My guest today is Dr. Sunita Sa. Dr. Saw is an associate professor at Cornell University's SC Johnson School of Management, where she studies ethics, authority, compliance and defiance, including questions such as why we comply with bad advice and how disclosure policies can backfire. Her research has been published in many academic journals and covered by
反叛、顺从、妥协意味着什么？顺从还是同意。当情况需要时，你又该如何增强自己的决心和说“不”的能力？欢迎收听《心理学热议》（Speaking of Psychology），这是美国心理学协会的旗舰播客，旨在探讨心理科学与日常生活之间的联系。我是金·米尔斯（Kim Mills）。我今天的嘉宾是苏妮塔·萨（Sunita Sah）博士。萨博士是康奈尔大学（Cornell University）SC 约翰逊商学院（SC Johnson School of Management）的副教授，她在该领域研究道德、权威、顺从与反叛，包括诸如我们为什么会顺从糟糕的建议以及披露政策如何会适得其反等问题。她的研究已发表在许多学术期刊上，并被

4
00:01:49.000 --> 00:02:30.000
media outlets, including the New York Times, the Harvard Business Review and Scientific American. Her new book is called Defy, the power of no in a world that demands yes. Dr. Saw, thank you for joining me today. It's wonderful to be here. Thank you, Kim. The Oxford English Dictionary defines defiance as to challenge the power of another person boldly and openly. But in your book, you offer an alternative definition. Can you tell us what that is and why you felt defiance needed a new definition? Absolutely. So I've been fascinated by what that single word defy means for a very long time.
各大媒体报道，包括《纽约时报》、《哈佛商业评论》和《科学美国人》。她的新书名为《反叛：在要求说“好的”世界中“不”的力量》（Defy: The Power of No in a World That Demands Yes）。萨博士，非常感谢您今天能来到节目中。很高兴能来到这里。谢谢你，金。牛津英语辞典将“反叛”定义为“大胆且公开地挑战另一个人的权力”。但在您的书中，您提出了一个替代的定义。您能告诉我们那是什么定义，以及为什么您觉得反叛需要一个新的定义吗？当然。事实上，我长期以来一直对“反叛（defy）”这个单一词汇的含义着迷。

5
00:02:30.000 --> 00:03:21.000
So as a child, I was known for being an obedient daughter and a student. and I remember asking my dad at one point, what does my name mean? And he told me, in Sanskrit, Sinita means good. And I mostly lived up to that. So I did what I was told. I did my homework as expected, went to school on time. I even had my hair cut the way my parents insisted. And these were the messages I received, you know, to be good, which means to be polite, to obey, to listen, not question authority. And many of the words, get these messages from a young age. And we start equating compliance with being good and defiance with being bad. But when I looked into it, and I've been studying this now for a very long time,
作为一个孩子，我因做个听话的女儿和学生而闻名。我记得有一次问我爸爸，我的名字是什么意思？他告诉我，在梵语中，Sinita 的意思是“好”。我基本上也一直是这样生活的。所以我做被要求做的事。按预期完成作业，按时去学校。甚至连发型都是按照父母坚持的那样去剪的。而我接收到的这些信息就是，你知道的，要听话，这意味着要礼貌、服从、听话、不质疑权威。许多人从很小的时候就接收到这些信息。我们开始将顺从等同于好，将反叛等同于坏。但当我深入研究时——而且我研究这个已经很长时间了——

6
00:03:21.000 --> 00:04:09.000
I saw that there were some problems with that in being so compliant. So, for example, one survey found that nine out of 10 healthcare workers, most of them nurses, did not feel comfortable speaking up when they saw a colleague making an error. And it's the same across industries. So in another survey of over 1,700 crew members of commercial airlines, about half of them felt uncomfortable saying something when they saw a mistake. So these could be life and death consequences. We want these people to be speaking up when they see an error or a mistake. And it made me start to wonder, is it sometimes bad to be so good? what do we sacrifice by being so compliant? And what I discovered now that I've been looking at this for years,
我发现过于顺从存在一些问题。例如，一项调查发现，十分之九的医护人员（其中大多数是护士）在看到同事犯错时，觉得不敢直言不讳。其他行业也是如此。在对 1,700 多名商业航空公司机组人员的另一项调查中，大约有一半人在看到错误时觉得不敢出言制止。这些可能会带来生死攸关的后果。我们希望这些人看到错误或失误时能够直言不讳。这让我开始怀疑，表现得这么好有时是不是一件坏事？我们因为过于顺从而牺牲了什么？而现在我研究了多年，我所发现的是，

7
00:04:09.000 --> 00:04:41.000
even if it's not life and death situations, when we don't speak up, when we see something wrong, it can be quite draining and have an effect on us. And I often felt a lot of tension between that aspect of doing what was expected of me and doing what I thought that was right. And that inspired so much of my research. And what I discovered that I think is crucial and really changed how I think is that we've misunderstood what it means to defy. So I'm not one to usually disagree with the Oxford English Dictionary.
即使不是生死攸关的情况，当我们看到不对劲的事情却选择保持沉默时，这也相当消耗精力，并会对我们产生影响。我经常在履行对我的期望这一方面和我认为正确的事情之间感受到很大的张力。这激发了我的许多研究。而我发现的、我认为至关重要且真正改变了我的思维方式的一点是，我们误解了反叛的真正含义。所以我通常不是那种会反驳《牛津英语辞典》的人。

8
00:04:41.000 --> 00:05:35.000
After all, I grew up in the UK. But I think that definition of defiance that you described is too narrow and doesn't honour our agency. And so my definition of defiance is that to defy means to act in accordance with your values when there is pressure to do otherwise. So it takes defiance and transforms it into a positive pro-social force for society. Because if you think about it, Kim, all our individual acts of consent and dissent every day add up to the society that we live in. And so it affects our lives, our workplaces, our communities. And that's why I'm so passionate about it and think that we need to understand what defiance really means and the power behind it. Now, as long as we're talking about language, let me ask you the difference between compliance and consent.
毕竟，我在英国长大。但我认为你刚才描述的那种反叛的定义过于狭隘，并没有尊重我们的自主性。因此，我对反叛的定义是：反叛意味着在面临相反的压力时，按照自己的价值观行事。因此，它将反叛转化为对社会的一种积极的、亲社会的正向力量。因为如果你仔细想想，金，我们每天所有的个体的顺从与异议行为，汇聚成了我们生活的社会。因此，它影响着我们的生活、我们的工作场所、我们的社区。这就是为什么我对这个课题充满热情，并认为我们需要理解反叛的真正含义及其背后的力量。既然我们在谈论语言，请允许我问你，顺从和同意有什么区别？

9
00:05:35.000 --> 00:06:12.000
Absolutely. Well, the two are often conflated, but they are actually quite different. So to comply with something just means to go along with something. And it's usually externally driven. Somebody is asking you to do something or there's expectations in your organization or in society. and is to go along and do comply with that expectation. Consent is not compliance. Consent is a thoroughly considered authorization that is an active expression of our deeply held values. So why do I say that?
当然。嗯，这两个词经常被混为一谈，但它们实际上非常不同。因此，顺从某事仅仅意味着随大流。而且它通常是由外部驱动的。有人要求你做某事，或者你的组织或社会中存在某种期望。而顺从就是随波逐流并顺从这种期望。同意不等同于顺从。同意是一种经过深思熟虑的授权，是我们深层价值观的积极表达。我为什么这么说呢？

10
00:06:12.000 --> 00:06:46.000
Well, I worked as a physician before I became a organizational psychologist, and what I do in my research is take informed consent, the definition of informed consent from medicine and apply it to other decisions that we make in our lives. So for informed consent, we need five elements. And these are, so first of all, capacity. We need to have the mental capacity to make a decision. So physicians assess patients for capacity. Is this patient too ill, are they under the influence of any drugs or alcohol?
嗯，在成为组织心理学家之前，我曾是一名医生，我在研究中所做的工作，是将医学中“知情同意”的定义应用到我们生活中做出的其他决策中。因此，对于知情同意，我们需要五个要素。它们是：首先，民事行为能力（capacity）。我们需要具备做出决定的精神能力。因此，医生会评估患者的决策能力。这个病人病得太重了吗？他们是否受到任何药物或酒精的影响？

11
00:06:46.000 --> 00:07:05.000
So first of all, hopefully we have the capacity to make a decision. And then next, we need knowledge. so we need information on the decision that we're about to make. But it's not just knowledge, it's not just receiving the information, it's understanding it, which is the third element. What are the risks? What are the benefits? What are the alternatives?
所以首先，希望我们具备做出决定的能力。接下来，我们需要知识（knowledge）。我们需要关于即将做出的决策的信息。但不仅仅是知识，不仅仅是接收信息，而是理解它，这是第三个要素。风险是什么？收益是什么？替代方案是什么？

12
00:07:05.000 --> 00:07:39.000
And to really understand that. Then the fourth one is this freedom to say no. Because if we don't have the freedom to say no, it's not really consent. It's simply compliance. And if those four elements are there, then you can engage in the fifth element, which is your authorization. So if you want to say yes with all those elements, you have the capacity, the knowledge, the understanding, the freedom to say no, then that's your informed consent. If you want to say no, that's your informed refusal.
并且要真正理解这一点。然后第四个要素是说“不”的自由。因为如果我们没有说“不”的自由，那就不是真正的同意。这仅仅是顺从。如果具备了这四个要素，那么你就可以参与第五个要素，也就是你的授权（authorization）。因此，如果你在具备所有这些要素的情况下想要说“好”——你具备能力、知识、理解力、说“不”的自由——那么这就是你的知情同意。如果你想说“不”，那就是你的知情拒绝。

13
00:07:39.000 --> 00:08:18.000
Why do most people find it so hard to say no even when we know we should, whether that's agreeing to an obligation we don't want or staying quiet when we see someone behaving unethically? In those moments, what is it that holds us back from speaking up? Well, we often actively resist defiance, and there's a number of reasons I've found for this, but one key reason is that we feel this enormous pressure to go along with other people, this social pressure. And I've seen this time and time again in my research experiment after experiment. So let me describe this type of pressure that I'm talking about.
为什么大多数人发现说“不”如此困难，即使我们知道自己应该这么做，无论是答应一个我们并不想要的义务，还是在看到某人表现出不道德行为时保持沉默？在这些时刻，是什么阻碍了我们直言不讳？嗯，我们经常积极地抵制反叛，我发现这有许多原因，但其中一个关键原因是，我们感到了顺从他人的巨大压力，这种社会压力。我在我的研究中一次又一次地看到了这一点，实验接连不断。所以让我来描述一下我所说的这种压力。

14
00:08:18.000 --> 00:08:44.000
And it could range from small-stakes situations to much larger ones. So when I was working as a junior doctor, my first job, I received an invitation to meet with a financial advisor for free. And I remember that meeting really well because it was in the posh meeting room at work. So as posh as you can get on the National Health Service, it was a room I didn't even know existed. It had a carpet. There was a couch.
它的范围可以从低风险的小事情到大得多的情况。因此，当我作为一名初级医生工作、担任我的第一份工作时，我收到了一份与财务顾问免费会面的邀请。我清楚地记得那次会议，因为它是在工作场所豪华的会议室里举行的。作为国民医疗服务体系（NHS）里能办到的最豪华的房间，那是一个我甚至不知道存在的房间。它铺着地毯。还有一张沙发。

15
00:08:44.000 --> 00:09:22.000
I was like, how come I've never discovered this before? So I went in after a long night on call and I sort sank down on the sofa and Dan, who was the advisor when he walked in, he was tall, he was Hansen, big smile, smart suit. And he greeted me warmly and he spent an hour talking with me. He asked many questions, mostly about my finances. He built up this amazing report. And after about an hour, he asked me whether he could write a report. he would send it in a week and he recommended that I invest in a couple of funds. All of this was for free.
当时我值完漫长的一夜班后走进去，整个人陷在沙发里。顾问丹（Dan）走进来时，他身材高大、英俊、带着灿烂的笑容、穿着笔挺的西装。他热情地迎接了我，花了一个小时和我交谈。他问了很多问题，大部分是关于我的财务状况。他建立了一种非常棒的融洽关系。大约一个小时后，他问我是否可以写一份报告。他会在一周内发给我，并建议我投资几只基金。所有这些都是免费的。

16
00:09:22.000 --> 00:09:53.000
So I was impressed and I had to ask him, what's in it for you? And he said, well, there's no such thing as a free lunch. And he would receive a commission if I invested in the funds that he was recommending. So he disclosed this conflict of interest. And what was key for me is that that disclosure did change the dynamics of the situation. So I did feel less trust in the advice. which is arguably the intended purpose of that disclosure. But at the same time, I now felt more pressure to go along with his advice
所以我印象深刻，不得不问他：这对你有什么好处？他说，天下没有免费的午餐。如果我投资了他推荐的基金，他将获得佣金。因此，他披露了这一利益冲突。对我和最关键的是，这种披露确实改变了情况的动态。因此，我对这些建议的信任确实减少了。可以说这也是这种披露的预期目的。但与此同时，我却感到了更大的压力，想要顺从他的建议，

17
00:09:53.000 --> 00:10:36.000
because I didn't want to signal that distrust to him. I didn't want him to think that this disclosure had now corrupted the lovely rapport that had been built up. And I started to feel really uncomfortable and I just then just wanted to sign and say, okay, fine, I'll invest in these ones. And what's happening there is the psychological process of insinuation anxiety. So this is a distinct type of anxiety that arises when we worry about our noncompliance with another person's wishes, is going to be interpreted as a signal of distressed and insinuates that the person is not whom they appear to be or should be,
因为我不想向他显露这种不信任。我不想让他认为这种披露已经破坏了我们之间建立的极好的融洽关系。我开始感到非常不舒服，只想签个字说：好吧，没问题，我投资这些就行了。而这里发生的是“暗示焦虑”（insinuation anxiety）的心理过程。这是一种独特类型的焦虑，当我们担心自己对他人意愿的不顺从将被解释为不信任的信号时就会产生，这暗示着这个人并非他们表面上看起来的那样，或者不应该是那样，

18
00:10:36.000 --> 00:11:12.000
that they're not trustworthy or competent or have integrity or benevolence. And it's an aversive emotional state. We become so concerned with offending the other person, we comply with them. Even in one-off situations I've found with strangers, we have a huge amount of compliance because we don't want to insinuate that our advisors, our co-workers, our friends are not trustworthy. So I'm sure we can imagine a situation where a boss is asking you to do something, everybody's going along with it, but you don't think it's right. But it's just too difficult to tell them that they're wrong, right?
暗示他们不值得信任、没有能力、缺乏正直或仁慈。这是一种厌恶性的情绪状态。我们变得如此担心冒犯对方，以至于我们选择顺从他们。我发现，即使在与陌生人的一锤子买卖中，我们也会表现出极高的顺从度，因为我们不想暗示我们的顾问、我们的同事、我们的朋友是不值得信任的。所以，我确信我们可以想象这样一种情景：老板要求你做某事，每个人都随大流，但你认为这并不对。然而，要告诉他们他们错了实在是太难了，对吧？

19
00:11:12.000 --> 00:11:49.000
or that we're at the hairdresser and we're sitting there and they're cutting away, cutting away, and you're thinking, no, that's not what I wanted. And it's just really hard to sort of tell them, no, this is not good. We normally just say, yes, thank you, and tip and walk out and feel dreadful. And so it happens in those everyday interactions. But what my research has shown me is that it's also in larger stake situations. So in medical scenarios, when your doctor disclosed, a conflict of interest, people feel more pressure, more insinuation anxiety to go along with it.
或者我们在美发沙龙里，坐在那里任由理发师剪啊剪，而你在想：不，这不是我想要的。要开口告诉他们“不，这不好”真的很难。我们通常只会说：“好的，谢谢”，然后给小费走人，内心感觉糟糕透顶。因此，这种情况发生在那些日常互动中。但我的研究向我表明，它也发生在风险更高的情境中。因此，在医疗场景中，当你的医生披露利益冲突时，人们会感到更大的压力，产生更多的暗示焦虑，从而选择顺从。

20
00:11:49.000 --> 00:12:29.000
So even though trust decreases, insinuation anxiety increases, and it depends on which force is greater as to whether they will take the advice or not and go along with that. So it's really important to know what that feeling is, this insinuation anxiety, and name it. Because once we name it, we know what we're dealing with and we can understand it. So what are some steps a person can take to overcome the pressure to comply? Are there things you can do in the short term and in the long term? And is this a kind of muscle you can train so it gets stronger? I absolutely believe that.
因此，尽管信任度下降了，但暗示焦虑却上升了，而人们最终是会采纳建议还是拒绝并顺从，取决于哪种力量更强大。因此，了解这种感觉——这种暗示焦虑——并将其命名是非常重要的。因为一旦我们给它命名了，我们就知道我们在应对什么，并且可以理解它。那么，一个人可以采取哪些步骤来克服顺从的压力呢？你在短期和长期内可以做些事情吗？这是一种你可以训练从而变得更强大的肌肉吗？我绝对相信这一点。

21
00:12:29.000 --> 00:13:13.000
So in the short term, I'll tell you what happened to me when Dan asked me to invest in those funds is when things were starting to just get that little bit too uncomfortable. and the silence was a little bit too much, I was thankfully saved by my beeper going off. And that was not planned, but I managed to leave the room. And he was like, no worries, I'll send you everything as planned. But when he did, I had time to think about it and think about my extremely limited disposable income. And, well, actually, I don't want to invest in these particular funds. And it was much easier for me to make that decision away from debt. So this is what I call the power of the pause.
所以在短期内，我告诉你当丹让我投资那些基金时发生了什么，当事情开始变得有点太不舒服时，

22
00:13:13.000 --> 00:13:40.000
If you can just pause rather than agreeing in the moment or on the spot, and especially if you can get some physical distance, that really helps decrease that signal of distrust. Because if this is social pressure, what we want to do is reduce that social interaction as much as we can. So if we can make the decision in private, if we have an opportunity to change our mind, in all my experiments I show that compliance goes down,
如果能够当场不急于答应，而是暂停一下，特别是如果你能拉开一些身体上的距离，那真的有助于减少这种不信任的信号。因为如果这是社会压力，我们想要做的就是尽可能减少这种社会互动。因此，如果我们能在私下做出决定，如果我们有机会改变主意，我在所有的实验中都表明，顺从度会下降，

23
00:13:40.000 --> 00:14:28.000
a huge amount. But making the decision in front of the other person is what leads us to comply a lot of the time and shows us that our public behavior is not in alignment with our private preferences. So we really need to be aware of that. But what's even better than just sort of thinking about the power of the pause in the moment is to really train ourselves for defiance. Because if you're like me and you had a master class in compliance growing up, you don't actually have the neural pathways to know how to defy it, and we need to train them. Because we know that training makes a big effect, right? Our brains have what we call neuroplasticity, so the neural pathways can change. If we're wired to comply, we need to change that wiring. So how can we do that?
