In the U.S., we think of ourselves as a nation of individualists. And is that self-image correct? Or are we just as likely to comply as people in other countries? I mean, the Milgram experiment was conducted, so 35 years after Milgram conducted in then early 1960s. And what people found was that obedience hadn't changed over that time, even though they were actually increased perceptions of agency and autonomy. So we do think of ourselves as possessing agency.
在美国，我们将自己视为一个个人主义国家。这种自我形象正确吗？还是我们和其他国家的人一样容易顺从？我的意思是，米尔格兰姆实验是在他进行实验大约 35 年后的 20世纪 60 年代初进行的。人们发现，在这期间，服从度并没有改变，尽管实际上人们对代理权（agency）和自主性的感知增强了。所以我们确实认为自己拥有代理权。

