00:26:46.000 --> 00:27:41.000
So two key questions that we want to ask. And the first one is, is it safe? So of course physically, but also maybe financially, psychologically, emotionally, we want to ask ourselves those questions. again, it can never be completely safe. So the question is really, is it safe enough? Is it safe enough? And then the second question is, is it effective? Will it be effective? Will I have some positive impact? And again, effective enough. Because some people don't really think about the positive impact because it's more important for them to take a stand for what they believe in, regardless of whether it has a positive impact or not. And so if we think, Think about like some of the really famous acts of defiance in history, like Rosa Parks. I mean, she was not safe to defy. She was living under segregation, under mortal threat at times.
也就是我们要问的两个关键问题。第一个是：它是安全的吗？当然是从身体上来说，但也可能在财务上、心理上、情感上，我们要问自己这些问题。再说一次，它永远不可能完全安全。所以真正的问题是：它够安全吗？它是否足够安全？然后第二个问题是：它有效吗？它会有效吗？我会产生一些积极的影响吗？同样，必须是足够有效。因为有些人并不真正考虑积极影响，因为对他们来说，为自己信仰的事情立场坚定更为重要，而不管它是否产生积极影响。因此，如果我们想想历史上一些真正著名的反叛行为，比如罗莎·帕克斯（Rosa Parks）。我的意思是，她反叛并不安全。她生活在种族隔离制度下，有时面临着致命的威胁。

41
00:27:41.000 --> 00:28:04.000
There was many consequences as well for her defiance that people don't talk about. It really had a huge impact on her. She lost her job. She was unemployed for 10 years. There were many consequences. But when she was asked 10 years later, would she do it again? She was a course, you know, and she had actually practiced for that moment. It wasn't a spontaneous decision like some people think.
人们没有谈论的，还有她的反叛带来的许多后果。它确实对她的生活产生了巨大的影响。她失去了工作。她失业了 10 年。有许多后果。但当她在 10 年后被问及她是否会再做一次时，她当然回答会，你知道的，她实际上为那一刻进行了练习。这并不像某些人想象的那样是一个冲动的决定。

42
00:28:04.000 --> 00:28:54.000
She had been practicing for that moment in years. And at that particular point, her defiance calculus, whether it was safe or effective enough, was a yes for her. Now, here's a question for parents and other caregivers. You don't want your toddler to be defiant every time you need to get them in their car seat. So how do you teach children to respect adults and other children, but also to stand up to themselves when necessary? Well, one of the things that I think we do for most children is train them in compliance. We train them in compliance a lot. And if we misunderstand what defiance actually means, which is acting in alignment with your values, some of them, especially when it comes to teens, and they're doing the exact opposite of what you tell them. No, I have a teenager right now.
她为那一刻练习了好几年。而在那个特定的时刻，她关于反叛的计算——它是否安全或足够有效——对她来说是一个肯定的答案。现在，这里有一个针对父母和其他照顾者的问题。你并不希望你的幼儿在你每次需要把他们放进安全座椅时都表现得叛逆。那么，你如何教导孩子尊重成年人和其他孩子，但在必要时也能挺身而出维护自己呢？嗯，我认为我们对大多数孩子做的事情之一，就是对他们进行顺从训练。我们对他们进行了大量的顺从训练。如果我们误解了反叛的真正含义，即按照你的价值观行事，他们中的一些人，特别是涉及到青少年时，就会做完全与你告诉他们相反的事情。不，我现在就有一个青少年。

43
00:28:54.000 --> 00:29:20.000
So some of these scenarios are very vivid in my mind, such as playing the Xbox, for example, and I would love him to do his homework instead. And so I ask him, are you going to do your homework? Well, and he's like, well, I was going to do my homework. But now that you've asked me to, I'm not going to do it. And so what does that show us? Is that being defiant? Well, actually, in my definition, no.
所以其中一些场景在我的脑海中非常生动，比如玩 Xbox，而我更希望他去做作业。所以我问他：你打算做作业吗？嗯，他会说：嗯，我本来打算做作业的。但既然你要求我做了，我就不做了。那么这向我们展示了什么？这叫反叛吗？事实上，根据我的定义，不是。

44
00:29:20.000 --> 00:29:50.000
because what he's doing is listening intently to what I'm saying and acting in the exact opposite direction, which means that it's what I call false defiance. But defiance like consent comes from within. It's not from an external source. It doesn't depend on anybody else's expectations. It's coming from within you. And so that's not really defiance. And what we need to do is actually train our children to,
因为他所做的是认真听取我说的话，并朝着完全相反的方向采取行动，这意味着我称之为“虚假反叛”（false defiance）。但反叛就像同意一样，是发自内心的。它不是来自外部源头。它不依赖于任何其他人的期望。它是从你内心深处发出的。所以这并不真的是反叛。我们需要做的是真正训练我们的孩子们去，

45
00:29:50.000 --> 00:30:40.000
be defined. If we think of defiance as a good, positive force, we want to give them training in that. So one of the ways this really hit home was when I was about six or seven years old and I was walking home with my mom from the grocery store. And my mom was wheeling the shopping cart, which was just like an old rickety cart on two wheels, a bit like wheel luggage that we take to the airports. And it had our produce in it. And it was quite a long walk back home. I grew up in Yorkshire in England, and we wanted to take a shortcut through what we call there a snicket. A snicket is just a very narrow alleyway, and we decided to go through that alleyway on the way home to reduce the length of our walk. Now, in that alleyway, we were confronted with about five or six teenage boys,
学会反叛。如果我们认为反叛是一种好的、积极的力量，我们希望在这方面对他们进行训练。这种感悟真正深入我心的情境之一，是我大约六七岁的时候，我和妈妈从杂货店步行回家。我妈妈推着购物车，那只是一辆装在两个轮子上的旧破车，有点像我们带到机场的带轮行李。里面装着我们的农产品。走回家是一段相当长的路。我在英格兰约克郡（Yorkshire）长大，我们想抄近路穿过我们在那里称为 snicket 的地方。snicket 只是一条非常狭窄的小巷，我们决定在回家的路上穿过那条小巷，以缩短步行距离。然而在那条小巷里，我们遇到了大约五六个十几岁的男孩，

46
00:30:40.000 --> 00:31:40.000
and they blocked our path, and they shouted out some obnoxious things to us and told us to go back Now, my mom is quite petite. She's 4'10 at the most. She was wearing her blue sari. She had her hair neatly platted back one single plat. And the way I thought about my mom, if we think about compliance and defiance as binary, which I've realized it is not, you know, there's these stages of defiance. But if we do think about it that way, I had very neatly put her in the compliant box because she served everybody's needs. She did the cooking, the cleaning, the grocery shopping. She was always looking after other people. She was not loud or aggressive. And until that day, I thought she was completely compliant. But when this happened and those boys stopped her, my reaction to them was instant. So I grabbed my mom's arm and I averted my gaze. I did not want to look at them.
他们挡住我们的去路，对我们大喊大叫一些令人讨厌的话，叫我们滚回去。现在，我妈妈个子很小。她最多四英尺十英寸（约 147 厘米）。她穿着蓝色的纱丽（sari）。她的头发整齐地编在脑后，梳成一条辫子。在我对妈妈的印象中，如果我们把顺从和反叛视为二进制的——我意识到它并不是，你知道的，反叛有这些不同的阶段——但如果我们确实那样看待它，我曾非常整齐地把她归入“顺从”的盒子里面，因为她满足了所有人的需求。她做饭、打扫卫生、买杂货。她总是照顾别人。她既不喧闹也不具侵略性。直到那天以前，我都认为她是完全顺从的。但当这件事发生、那些男孩拦住她时，我对他们的反应是本能的。所以我抓住了妈妈的胳膊，移开了视线。我不想看着他们。

47
00:31:40.000 --> 00:32:03.000
And I just wanted to maneuver as fast as possible through them to go home. and she stopped and she looked at them straight in the eye and she said, What do you mean? And my heart started racing. I grabbed her arm even tighter and I whispered to her, no, come on, Ma. And she shrugged me off. She said no.
我只想以最快的速度从他们中间穿过去回家。而她停了下来，直视着他们的眼睛说：“你们什么意思？”我的心开始狂跳。我把她的胳膊抓得更紧了，对她低声说：“不，走吧，妈妈。”她甩开我的手臂。她说：“不。”

48
00:32:03.000 --> 00:32:22.000
And then she got the shopping cart. She put it up upright and she put one hand on her hip. And she looked back at the boys again and she said, What do you mean? And the boys didn't say anything. They were looking at each other. And she said, oh, yeah, you think you're so big and strong, big tough boys, right? Clever boys.
然后她拿了购物车。她把它扶正，一只手叉在腰上。她又回头看了看那几个男孩，说：“你们什么意思？”男孩们什么也没说。他们互相看着。她说：“哦，是啊，你们觉得自己又大又强壮，了不起的硬汉对吧？聪明的男孩。”

49
00:32:22.000 --> 00:32:54.000
And again, there was complete silence from their side. And then one of the boys looked at the other and said, let's go. And they just dispersed. And I was astounded because I never expected that from my mom that day. And she grabbed the shopping cart and then she started walking back home really fast. and I kind of stood frozen for a while before I ran after her. But what that really showed me was a couple of things. One is that defiance is not a personality, it's a practice.
对方依然是一片沉默。接着，其中一个男孩看着另一个说：“我们走吧。”然后他们就散开了。我感到很震惊，因为那天我从我妈妈身上完全没料到这一点。她抓起购物车，然后开始飞快地走回家里。我在她后面追上去之前，在原地呆住了一会儿。但这件事真正向我展示了几点。第一，叛逆不是一种性格，而是一种实践。

50
00:32:54.000 --> 00:33:20.000
It's a skill set that we can choose to use or not. And it became clear to me that she had encountered those boys before, but that day she did something different. She must have been practicing for it, or perhaps I was there in that moment, and she wanted to show me something. And that day she did something different. So even if we're being conditioned to comply the way I was, that I'm sure the way that my mother was as well,
它是一套我们可以选择使用或不使用的技能。我很清楚，她以前遇到过那些男孩，但那天她做了一些不同的事情。她一定一直在为此练习，或者也许我当时就在场，她想向我展示点什么。那天她做了一些不同的事情。所以，即使我们被训练去顺从——就像我一样，我相信我母亲过去也是这样——

51
00:33:20.000 --> 00:33:49.000
compliance might be our default, but it's not our destiny. The second thing that it showed me is that defiance might change you. The instances where I've been able to defy, it is transformational. We feel more authentic, more honest. It's more joyful life to be able to live in alignment with your values. but it also changes the people that observe it. I mean, my mom's defiance definitely had an impact on me. It stayed with me.
顺从可能是我们的默认状态，但它并不是我们的宿命。它向我展示的第二件事是，叛逆可能会改变你。我能够进行叛逆的那些时刻具有变革性的意义。我们会感到更加真实、更加诚实。能够按照自己的价值观生活，是更加快乐的人生。但它也会改变那些观察它的人。我是说，我妈妈的叛逆绝对对我有影响。它留在了我心中。

52
00:33:49.000 --> 00:34:43.000
And it's what I call this ripple effect or the defiance domino effect is that it inspires other people to also go along with their values instead of going along with other people. And that's why it's really important because if we want to train our children to be defiant, these moments are what creates society. And what I really hope for with my book and making to find successful and a changing our understanding is that one day, one of those teens would have spoken up and said, stop doing that to his friends. So my immigrant mother wouldn't have to. So just to close, let me ask you, what are the big questions you still want to answer? What's next for you in research? Well, I am researching what I call the voice empathy gap. So I've interviewed over 50 nurses and nurse managers.
这就是我所说的涟漪效应或叛逆多米诺骨牌效应，它激励其他人也去坚持自己的价值观，而不是随波逐流。这就是它非常重要的地方，因为如果我们想训练我们的孩子敢于叛逆，这些时刻就是塑造社会的原因。我对我这本书的真正期望，以及让反抗变得成功并改变我们的理解，是希望有一天，其中一个青少年会站出来对他的朋友说：“别那样做。”这样我的移民母亲就不用出头了。所以最后，请允许我问你，你仍然想解答的重大问题是什么？你在研究方面的下一步计划是什么？嗯，我正在研究我所说的心声同理心鸿沟。所以，我已经采访了50多名护士和护士长。

53
00:34:43.000 --> 00:35:19.000
And what I've uncovered is that when you ask nurses why they don't speak up in a situation at work, when they see some errors, the reasons that they give are very different from why their nurse managers think that they don't speak up. So there is this gap in how it's viewed in that the nurses don't think it's safe or effective enough. And the nurse managers think it's to do with the characteristics of the nurses, is that they're not taking responsibility for things that go wrong or that they just don't have the ability or the skill set or the confidence to speak up. Looking into interventions to decrease that gap,
我发现的是，当你问护士为什么在工作看到一些错误时不在场开口反映，他们给出的原因与他们的护士长认为他们不开口反映的原因有很大不同。因此，在如何看待这一问题上存在这种鸿沟，护士们认为这不够安全或不够有效。而护士长则认为这与护士的性格有关，是她们没有为出错的事情承担责任，或者她们根本没有开口说话的能力、技能或自信。我正在研究缩小这一鸿沟的干预措施，

54
00:35:19.000 --> 00:35:39.000
because if we have a different understanding about what's causing people to speak up, then we have some kind of status quo where the environment and the culture doesn't change. And that's really important for us to manage if we want to create the right types of culture for people to speak up. Well, Dr. Saw, I want to thank you for joining me today.
